Chapter: FsSpawnInherits
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem

 BACKLOG 1.14, the spawn half: a child inherits its parent's FileSystem
 grant and reaches the disk only through the kernel servicer, because it
 holds no Device.Block of its own. This mirrors scope-runtime-spawn, but
 the write goes through the bare FileSystem effect op (the
 compiler-installed servicer) rather than fat16-* directly, so it pins
 that the servicer path -- not just the Device.Block path -- survives a
 fork: the write cap travelled with the child, and elevation reached the
 disk on its behalf.

 The parent is scoped to "OK" and does nothing itself. The child performs
 an in-scope write-file and reports the result: ten if it landed, zero if
 it did not. Ten is the only pass.

   10  the write landed. The FileSystem.Write grant came with the child
       and the servicer elevated on its behalf though it holds no block
       device.
    0  the write did not land: the cap did not travel, or the servicer
       does not work in a spawned process.

 An out-of-scope write is NOT attempted here: write-file is an effect op,
 so an out-of-scope path literal is a compile error (CDX4002), not a
 runtime refusal. The runtime scope path is pinned by scope-runtime-deny;
 this test pins cap inheritance and the servicer surviving a spawn.

 The effectful call sits in argument position rather than in a let: a let
 bound to a value with a concrete effect row is CDX2033, and poke-byte is
 itself effectful. Nest, do not bind.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Concurrent, Console, FileSystem.Write "OK"] Integer
  opening = act
    pid <- process-spawn (\x -> poke-byte 28000 0 (if write-file "OK.TXT" "child data" then 10 else 0))
    let y = process-yield
    in peek-byte 28000 0
  end
